<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.9.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02475</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.9.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>26d7ccde-67fa-4f0d-837a-f865888c5292</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which of the following points is the vertex of the quadratic function \(f(x)=x^2+8x+22\)?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \( (8, 22) \)</item>
  <item>\( (0, 22) \)</item>
  <item>\( (-4, 6) \)</item>
  <item>\( (4, 6) \)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((-4, 6)\)</para>
<para>\((\frac{b}{2})^2=(\frac{8}{2})^2=16\)
\(f(x)=x^2+8x+16+22-16\)
\(f(x)=(x+4)^2+6\)
Vertex: \((-4, 6)\)</para>
    
<para>For questions 2–4, use the function \(f(x)=(x-4)(x+2)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write the function in standard form. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(f(x)=x^2-2x-6\)</para>
<para>\(f(x)=(x-4)(x+2)\)
\(f(x)=x^2-4x+2x-6\)
\(f(x)=x^2-2x-6\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write the function from question 2 in vertex form. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(f(x)=(x-1)^2-7\)</para>
<para>\(f(x)=x^2-2x+1-6-1\)
\(f(x)=x^2-2x+1-7\)
\(f(x)=(x-1)^2-7\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Identify the vertex of the quadratic function \(f(x) = (x-1)^2 - 9\).</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \( (1, -9) \)</item>
  <item>\( (1, 9) \)</item>
  <item>\( (-1, 9) \)</item>
  <item>\( (-9, -1) \)</item>
</list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((1, -9)\)
  </para><para>The function is written in vertex form:
    \(f(x) = (x - h)^2 + k\)
    \(f(x) = (x - 1)^2 - 9\)
    \(h = 1\) and \(k = -9\)
    The vertex is \((1, -9)\).
  </para>
<para>For questions 5 - 6, write an equation in vertex form of the quadratic function that has a vertex at \((-4, 1)\) and also goes through the point \((6, -1)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the value of \(a\) for vertex form in simplest form.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(\frac{-1}{50}\)</para>
<para>\(-1=a(6-(-4))^2+(1)\)
\(-1=a(10)^2+1\)
\(-2=100a\)
\(a=\frac{-2}{100}=\frac{-1}{50}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write the equation in vertex form. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=\frac{-1}{50}(x+4)^2+1\)</para>
<para>For questions 7 and 8, write an equation in vertex form of the quadratic function that has a vertex at \((-2, -3)\) and also goes through the point \((4, 2)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the value of \(a\) for vertex form in simplest form.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(\frac{5}{36}\)</para>
<para>\(2=a(4-(-2))^2+(-3)\)
\(2=a(6)^2+(-3)\)
\(2+3=36a+(-3)+3\)
\(5=36a\)
\(a=\frac{5}{36}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write the equation in vertex form. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=\frac{5}{36}(x+2)^2-3\)</para>
    
<para>For questions 9 - 10, use the function \(f(x)=x^2\) that is shifted to create the function \(g(x)\) that has a vertex at \((-4, 6)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write \(g(x)\) in vertex form. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(g(x)=(x+4)^2+6\)</para>
<para>Substitute the vertex in for \((h, k)\)  
\(g(x)=(x-(-4))^2+6\)  
  \(g(x)=(x+4)^2+6\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item> Write \(g(x)\) in standard form.  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(g(x)=x^2-6x+7\)</para>
<para>Multiply the binomials then simplify.
\(g(x)=(x+4)^2+6\)
\(g(x)=(x+4)(x+4)+6\)
\(g(x)=x^2+4x+4x+16+6\)
\(g(x)=x^2-6x+7\)</para>
</section>
